# The national unit for japan. Items take n=2; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g4-japan-pages-of-our-history v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=9 subject=history nation=japan levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Keeping safe from earthquakes"
  goal "Put Japan's great earthquakes and what was learnt from them in order, and say why every school practises for one."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=1900 to=2020 step=20 years=[1923, 1960, 1995] labels=["Great Kanto earthquake", "Disaster Prevention Day begins", "Great Hanshin earthquake"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      Japan has many earthquakes. On 1 September 1923 the great Kanto earthquake shook Tokyo and Yokohama, and fires spread through the wooden houses. In 1960 Japan made 1 September Disaster Prevention Day, when schools and towns practise what to do. In 1995 the Great Hanshin earthquake struck Kobe, and people learnt again how neighbours can help each other.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n4-what n=2
    }
    show history.n4-order n=2 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n4-between n=2 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n4-century n=2 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.source-hand k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.bc-ad-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.money-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n4-order n=2 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n4-what n=2
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n4-century n=2 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.source-hand k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n4-between n=2 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n4-why n=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n4-find-out n=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Every date in our country's story can be put on the line and counted from, and every one came from a source."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to use the grade's methods (timelines across year one, centuries, sources) on a
    chapter of the family's own country. The way in names what a thing was; the core orders three
    events, counts the years between two, names a century, and reviews sources, BC and AD and the
    story of money; the two-star question asks why something was done. If the child is stuck on
    counting, count to the next hundred first. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you. Japan's 社会 for grades three and four asks children to study how their region keeps people safe, including from natural disasters (the course of study as read in its 2008 edition lists the region's safety among the grade's topics; the 2017 edition names 自然災害 directly). This lesson touches disasters in which many people died; it is told through what people did to be ready.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the thing was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions are the plainest."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
